24	\begin{abstract}
25	Web-scraped corpora increasingly contain multi-generational synthetic text produced by uncontrolled model$\to$data$\to$model feedback loops.
26	Existing detectors classify text as real or synthetic but discard information about \emph{how many recursive cycles} the text has undergone---a distinction that matters because first-generation synthetic data can be beneficial while deeply recursive text drives distribution collapse.
27	We formalize \textbf{ordinal generation depth estimation} as a new task, construct a benchmark spanning three model families (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, GPT-2~XL) $\times$ three decoding strategies (nucleus, top-$k$, temperature) $\times$ six depths (0--5), and systematically characterize the fundamental limits of depth estimability.
28	Our primary contributions are: (1)~the \textbf{Depth Degradation Atlas}, a comprehensive empirical map of how statistical features evolve across recursive depths, and (2)~the \textbf{Effective Discriminable Depth (EDD)} metric, which we find equals~2 in 7 of 9 model--strategy combinations---meaning that beyond the second generation, adjacent depths become statistically indistinguishable.
29	We evaluate multiple estimation approaches including a contrastive ordinal learning method (DCOL), finding that no method significantly outperforms simple ordinal baselines (DCOL 42\% vs.\ CORAL 40\% 6-class accuracy, $p=0.12$), confirming the bottleneck lies in signal availability rather than learning methodology.
30	Cross-model transfer accuracy drops to 33\%, revealing that depth signals are model-specific and that practical deployment requires model fingerprinting as a prerequisite.
31	These findings reframe recursive depth estimation from a classification problem to a measurement science problem, establishing that the actionable distinction is ternary: human-written vs.\ first-generation vs.\ deeply recursive text (73\% accuracy).
32	\end{abstract}
