35	\section{Introduction}
36	
37	The proliferation of large language model (LLM) generated content on the web has created a new challenge for training data curation: \emph{recursive contamination}.
38	When models are trained on data that includes their own outputs (or outputs of predecessor models), each generation cycle progressively degrades the statistical properties of the text \citep{shumailov2024ai,drayson2025model}.
39	This recursive feedback loop is not hypothetical---web crawls already contain substantial synthetic content, and each new generation of models trained on such crawls compounds the effect.
40	
41	Existing work addresses the \emph{detection} problem: distinguishing human-written from machine-generated text \citep{mitchell2023detectgpt,bao2024fastdetectgpt,tulchinskii2024diveye,deng2024editlens}.
42	However, detection is binary, while the recursive contamination problem is inherently \emph{ordinal}: first-generation synthetic text may be nearly indistinguishable from human text and even beneficial for training, whereas deeply recursive text exhibits measurable quality degradation.
43	The critical question is not ``is this synthetic?'' but ``how many recursive cycles has this undergone?''
44	
45	We formalize this as the \textbf{ordinal generation depth estimation} task and construct a controlled benchmark to study it systematically.
46	Rather than proposing a method and demonstrating superiority, our primary contribution is \emph{characterizing the fundamental limits} of depth estimation.
47	Through the \textbf{Depth Degradation Atlas}---a systematic mapping of how 19 statistical features evolve across depths, models, and decoding strategies---we establish the \textbf{Effective Discriminable Depth (EDD)}, the depth beyond which adjacent generations become statistically indistinguishable.
48	
49	Our key findings are:
50	\begin{enumerate}
51	    \item \textbf{EDD\,$=$\,2 is the dominant regime}: In 7 of 9 model--strategy combinations, reliable discrimination saturates at depth~2. Beyond this, recursive generation enters a statistical attractor where text properties fluctuate without systematic directional change.
52	    \item \textbf{Depth signals are model-specific}: Cross-model transfer accuracy is 33\% (vs.\ 42\% within-model), and feature importance rankings differ substantially across models (Kendall's $\tau = 0.52$). Practical depth estimation requires knowing which model generated the text.
53	    \item \textbf{The actionable granularity is ternary}: The distinction human / first-generation / deeply recursive achieves 73\% accuracy and captures nearly all practical value, with downstream curation performance within 0.8~pp of fine-grained 6-class estimation.
54	\end{enumerate}
55	
56	These results reframe recursive depth estimation from a classification engineering problem to a measurement science problem.
57	We release the benchmark, Atlas visualizations, and all evaluation code to support future work on this new task.\footnote{Code and data: \texttt{[anonymized for review]}}
58	
59	% ============================================================
60	\section{Related Work}
61	
62	\paragraph{AI-Generated Text Detection.}
63	Binary detection methods include statistical approaches based on log-probability curvature \citep{mitchell2023detectgpt}, fast detection via conditional probability divergence \citep{bao2024fastdetectgpt}, feature-based classifiers using diversity metrics \citep{tulchinskii2024diveye}, and calibrated detection via binocular scoring \citep{hans2024binoculars}.
64	EditLens \citep{deng2024editlens} extends beyond binary classification to estimate the extent of AI editing, but targets single-pass edits rather than recursive depth.
65	All of these approaches answer ``is this AI-generated?'' rather than ``how many recursive cycles has this undergone?''
66	
67	\paragraph{Model Collapse and Recursive Degradation.}
68	\citet{shumailov2024ai} demonstrated that iterative training on model outputs leads to irreversible distribution collapse, with later generations losing tail information.
69	\citet{drayson2025model} provided a systematic empirical characterization of model collapse patterns across architectures and identified non-monotonic degradation in certain configurations.
70	These studies assume depth is known \emph{a priori} and characterize what happens at each depth; we ask whether depth can be \emph{inferred} from text alone.
71	
72	\paragraph{Ordinal Classification.}
73	CORAL \citep{cao2020rank} introduced consistent rank logits for ordinal regression.
74	CLOC \citep{dai2025cloc} extended contrastive learning to ordinal settings with learnable margins.
75	We adapt CLOC to text depth estimation (DCOL) but find that in our setting, the learning objective contributes minimally compared to feature availability.
76	
77	% ============================================================
78	\section{Task Formalization and Benchmark}
79	
80	\subsection{Ordinal Depth Estimation}
81	
82	Given a text passage $x$, we define its \emph{generation depth} $d(x) \in \{0, 1, 2, \ldots, K\}$ where $d=0$ denotes human-written text and $d=k$ denotes text produced by the $k$-th recursive generation cycle.
83	The task is to predict $d(x)$ from $x$ alone, respecting the ordinal structure: confusing depth~1 with depth~3 is worse than confusing depth~1 with depth~2.
84	
85	\subsection{Benchmark Construction}
86	
87	We construct recursive generation chains using three model families and three decoding strategies:
88	
89	\paragraph{Models.} Pythia-1.4B (trained on The Pile), OLMo-1B (trained on Dolma), GPT-2~XL 1.5B (trained on WebText). These span different training corpora, architectures, and tokenizers.
90	
91	\paragraph{Decoding Strategies.} Nucleus sampling ($p=0.95$), top-$k$ sampling ($k=50$), and temperature sampling ($T=0.8$). Each strategy produces different distributional characteristics in generated text.
92	
93	\paragraph{Chain Construction.} Starting from human-written seed texts from each model's training domain, we generate depth-1 text by prompting the model with the seed, then generate depth-2 by prompting with depth-1 output, continuing to depth~5. This yields $3 \times 3 \times 6 = 54$ conditions.
94	
95	\paragraph{Features.} We extract 19 statistical features from each text passage: perplexity statistics (mean, variance, p10, p50, p90), surprisal statistics (mean, variance, p10, p50, p90), lexical diversity metrics (type-token ratio, Yule's K, vocab richness, hapax ratio), distributional shape features (zipf slope, burstiness), and structural features (mean sentence length, sentence length variance, repetition rate).
96	
